BLOCKED: order is hash pass1 -> spawn -> hash pass2 -> compare. On Windows every stage holds the file open (each hash read; the running image stays mapped), so a mutation can only land between child exit and pass2's open — sub-millisecond now the broker settles on its completed frame. My earlier 4s/20s/30s window measurements were of the defect d7b352e1 removed.
Measured: (1) in-place append @5ms -> PROCESS_BOUNDARY_BROKER_REFUSED, the write handle collides with image load. (2) Rotating two complete runnable images (node.exe copies differing in trailing bytes) -> same collision. (3) Rotation + bounded retries asserting drift observed AND every other attempt was exactly that collision: 8 attempts gave BROKER_REFUSED x4, OBSERVATION_INVALID x3, zero drifts — the latter honest, both passes hashed the same variant. (4) 1ms tick -> still none.

DECISION NEEDED, all plan changes:
(a) accept the arm as proven by the mutation drill (reddens when the digestsOf before!==after compare is deleted) plus code review, drop the runtime case;
(b) prove it at unit level against an injected fs — forbidden on this public surface by the task premise, so it needs its own module and plan;
(c) keep bounded retries tolerating launch collisions (on disk now) — honest but a race, cannot meet QA's "green 5 consecutive runs".

Tree: only the two owned paths, no residue.

